01:00:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re making a really sharp point, and it gets to the heart of the metaphor. Let me reframe the argument to show why the baking analogy actually supports the original answer—but also highlights where you’re right. ### The baking analogy, step by step **You said:** The recipe is the same ingredients and steps; the oven just interprets them differently (electric vs. gas). So why can’t game code be the same? In baking, the recipe consists of *oven‑independent* instructions like “bake at 350°F for 30 minutes.” The oven type doesn’t change the instruction because both ovens understand temperature and time. The recipe doesn’t have separate instructions for electric vs. gas – it just says “oven.” Now, **imagine a world where electric ovens require you to press a button labelled “BAKE” and gas ovens require you to turn a knob to a specific mark that doesn’t correspond to 350°F.** Suddenly, your recipe would need two different sets of instructions: - Electric: “Press BAKE, set thermostat to 350.” - Gas: “Turn knob to the ‘Moderate’ symbol, then light the pilot.” This is exactly what happens with game platforms. **They don’t share a universal “oven” interface.** - Windows uses DirectX for graphics. - PlayStation uses Gnm. - Nintendo Switch uses NVN. These are not “interpretations” of the same instruction – they are completely different languages for telling the hardware to draw a triangle. There is no common “draw triangle” command that all platforms understand natively. ### So when is the baking analogy correct? When you use a **cross‑platform game engine** (like Unity or Unreal), that engine acts as the universal oven. You write one set of instructions (“draw triangle at these coordinates”) and the engine translates it into whatever the specific platform needs. In that case, you truly have one recipe that works everywhere. But if you write the game without an engine, you must write the platform‑specific instructions yourself – just as you’d have to write a separate gas‑oven instruction if no universal oven existed. ### The core of your insight You’re right that **at the conceptual level, the game is the same.** A triangle drawn on Windows and a triangle drawn on PlayStation are identical in user experience. The “recipe” (game logic, rules, assets) is shared. But the **instructions for the hardware** (the platform‑specific API calls) are not the same – and they cannot be “interpreted automatically” without an abstraction layer. So to answer your original question more precisely: > **Why can’t we just copy and paste code from one platform to another?** **Because the hardware and operating system don’t share a common “oven language.”** You either: - Write the platform‑specific parts separately (rewriting), or - Use an engine that already has those parts written for you (no rewriting needed). The baking analogy holds only if you have an abstract recipe that works for any oven. Without that abstraction, you have to write a different “oven instruction” for each platform – and that’s exactly what we call rewriting.
